														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-4547647">
									

<p>Ooh, I think I get this one.</p>
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">[+]&nbsp;reveal&nbsp;possibly&nbsp;erroneous&nbsp;theory</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">[-]&nbsp;hide&nbsp;theory</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<p>So this apartment seems to be non-anomalous, but the Foundation is giving it some significance by noting it in a formal SCP document. Schroder here seems to be related to the apartment, given his body's retrieval is featured in the document, although there is no explicit mention of him having any connections with the apartment. The article also mentions a project he completed in late 2017, although the document states that he was &quot;unable or unwilling to describe what he had created&quot;.</p>
<p>Putting all of this together, I think it's pretty clear Schroder made the apartment, or at least granted it the certain quality which made it so notable as to be in an SCP document, and that this is the late 2017 project. Given that Schroder's oeuvre is described as &quot;defying advertisement and marketing&quot;, I'd say his goal in making the piece was to depict a frank version of real life. However, instead of something mundane being granted importance by being in a museum or other context (think Duchamp's <em><a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fountain_(Duchamp)">Fountain</a></em>), the apartment grants itself importance. This is Schroder's response to &quot;what he perceived as increasing commodification and consumerism within the art world&quot;: making something mundane with no embellishment.</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>
<p>Anyway, that got a bit rambly, but nevertheless, I very much enjoyed this SCP. Enthusiastic +1!</p>

