			<div class="content" id="post-content-4558336">
									

<p>I'm glad you enjoyed it and thanks to both of you for the thoughtful posts. I consider any of my SCPs making one person think to be successful.</p>
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">reply</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">–&nbsp;hide&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<p>Both of you are close to what I intended.</p>
<p>The &quot;anomaly&quot; is something that is not only mundane, but <em>cannot</em> be anything other than mundane, in both properties and description. This is also why nothing in the description explicitly links it to the Foundation or to the artist.</p>
<p>This is where the &quot;anti-art&quot; comes in, with the intention being a work of protest. I like barredowl's explanation but it is probably more sophisticated that what I was doing.</p>
<p>The question is whether it is possible to create something which is really unremarkable in any way, and in this case the answer was no, since it was so unremarkable it was special (hence why it is an SCP). This, and the apartment being advertised to the wealthy, in contravention of the artist's intent, led to the alcohol-related death.</p>
<p>The stuff in the missed details is spot on, except the luggage was only added in because the only picture I found had a luggage stand.</p>
<p>The number is meant to be just a normal apartment number (if you think it's too big I guess you could interpret it as Apartment 59/19 Whatever St, though I'm not sure how buildings would be numbered in Germany). It was only picked because I had 3919 and 4919 as building/architecture-related SCPs and get a kick out of continuing the chain.</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>

